// Sign-off tags applied at Marc103's explicit instruction on 11/13/26.
// Source excerpts below are verbatim; prose explains behavior and timing.
// Reviewed: fsm_fft_writer.sv and the fft_adder_core output-update contract.
// Writer RTL SHA-256:
// 086e12ad44eecf544313e1e05bb3520992107154f5f3f65a4a08e136bcc629fc
// T means the rising edge accepting valid_i while IDLE and outside reset.
// Commands after an edge refer to values after nonblocking assignments settle.
// A synchronous memory samples the preceding cycle's command at the next edge.

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 01. Scope and interface contract
- Serialize each completed butterfly into two writes on one sample-memory port:
  Y1 goes to B first, followed by the saved Y0 going to A one clock later.
- DATA_WIDTH is the full memory-word width. When a word stores a complex sample,
  pack its real/imaginary fields consistently on y0_i, y1_i and the memory port.
- The writer copies bits without arithmetic, rounding, format checks or masking.
  ABFP signs, zero encodings and NaN/Inf payloads therefore pass through unchanged.
- One clock domain, no backpressure and no transaction queue. The receiving
  memory must accept a write on every rising edge for which wr_en_o is high.
- The caller aligns addresses with the result strobe. This module does not
  calculate FFT addresses, delay addresses through the arithmetic pipeline,
  or track downstream completion and read/write memory dependencies.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 02. Parameters, ports and width checks
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
module fsm_fft_writer #(
    parameter DATA_WIDTH,
    parameter ADDR_WIDTH
) (
    input                              clk_i,
    input                              rst_i,

    input  [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         a_wr_addr_i,
    input  [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         b_wr_addr_i,
    input                              valid_i,
    input  [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         y0_i,
    input  [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         y1_i,

    output [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         wr_addr_o,
    output [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]         wr_data_o,
    output                             wr_en_o
);
"
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
    initial begin
        if(DATA_WIDTH < 1) $fatal(1, "DATA_WIDTH must be positive");
        if(ADDR_WIDTH < 1) $fatal(1, "ADDR_WIDTH must be positive");
    end
"
- Both widths must be positive, including the supported one-bit minimum.
- Every address assignment preserves exactly ADDR_WIDTH bits and every data
  assignment preserves exactly DATA_WIDTH bits; no resizing or address arithmetic
  occurs. There are no derived width or latency helpers needed by this FSM.
- rst_i controls cancellation; wr_data_o supplies the selected result to memory.
  The clock stays a clock; input addresses/data have no unconditional register bank.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 03. Current values and corresponding next values
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
    // Current values.
    typedef enum logic {IDLE, WRITE_A} state_t;
    state_t                            state;
    logic                              valid_previous;
    logic                              wr_en;
    logic [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          wr_addr;
    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          wr_data;
    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          y0_saved;

    // Corresponding next values.
    state_t                            state_next;
    logic                              valid_previous_next;
    logic                              wr_en_next;
    logic [ADDR_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          wr_addr_next;
    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          wr_data_next;
    logic [DATA_WIDTH - 1 : 0]          y0_saved_next;
"
- All six current variables have a matching next variable of the same type/width.
- IDLE accepts a new result. WRITE_A marks the one pending A/Y0 command.
- valid_previous detects the sampled low-to-high transition of valid_i.
- wr_addr, wr_data and wr_en hold the registered memory command. y0_saved holds
  the first edge's Y0 until the second command is issued.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 04. Clocked register bank and output wiring
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
    always @(posedge clk_i) begin
        state          <= state_next;
        valid_previous <= valid_previous_next;
        wr_en          <= wr_en_next;
        wr_addr        <= wr_addr_next;
        wr_data        <= wr_data_next;
        y0_saved       <= y0_saved_next;
    end

    assign wr_addr_o = wr_addr;
    assign wr_data_o = wr_data;
    assign wr_en_o = wr_en && !rst_i;
"
- All state/command registers update from their next values with nonblocking
  assignments. Data and address appear together after the same rising edge.
- The memory samples the command present before its rising edge. A B/Y1 command
  produced after T is consumed at T+1, before the register changes to A/Y0.
- wr_en_o is additionally masked directly by rst_i. The FSM resets synchronously,
  but write-enable suppression does not wait for that state-reset edge.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 05. Defaults, holds and reset
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
    always_comb begin
        state_next          = state;
        valid_previous_next = valid_i;
        wr_en_next          = 0;
        wr_addr_next        = wr_addr;
        wr_data_next        = wr_data;
        y0_saved_next       = y0_saved;

        if(rst_i) begin
            state_next          = IDLE;
            valid_previous_next = 0;
        end else begin
"
- Defaults assign every next variable on every combinational path; no latches
  are needed. Address/data hold unless a command is explicitly issued.
- Enable defaults low each cycle. The two active FSM actions override it high.
- Reset sets IDLE, clears sampled-valid history and leaves wr_en_next low.
  wr_addr, wr_data and y0_saved hold; their reset values are not forced to zero.
- Assert reset across a rising edge before use. Data/address may be unknown
  before their first qualified captures; consumers must obey wr_en_o.
- A high valid_i on the first edge after reset can be accepted because reset
  cleared valid_previous. It must describe a fresh, correctly aligned bundle.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 06. First command: accept the result and capture B/Y1
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
                IDLE: begin
                    if(valid_i && !valid_previous) begin
                        wr_addr_next  = b_wr_addr_i;
                        wr_data_next  = y1_i;
                        wr_en_next    = 1;
                        y0_saved_next = y0_i;
                        state_next    = WRITE_A;
                    end
                end
"
- Outside reset, IDLE accepts when valid_i is high and its previous sampled
  value was low. This is a sampled rising strobe, not a continuously high request.
- At T, B and Y1 are captured for the first output command. Y0 is captured at
  the same edge for later use. a_wr_addr_i is not read by this action.
- Consequently, B, Y0 and Y1 must all belong to the same butterfly at T.
  A can still be unavailable at that edge without affecting the first write.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 07. Second command: use the arriving A address and saved Y0
// Source: fsm_fft_writer.sv
"
                WRITE_A: begin
                    wr_addr_next = a_wr_addr_i;
                    wr_data_next = y0_saved;
                    wr_en_next   = 1;
                    state_next   = IDLE;
                end
                default: state_next = IDLE;
"
- At T+1, WRITE_A samples the current a_wr_addr_i and uses the Y0 saved at T.
  The current y0_i, y1_i and b_wr_addr_i do not determine this second command.
- A must belong to the accepted butterfly at T+1. It may arrive exactly one
  cycle after B, provided ordinary setup/hold timing is met at that sampling edge.
- The second command is issued regardless of valid_i; a one-clock result strobe
  is enough to launch both writes. Reset is the cancellation path.
- The state returns to IDLE after this edge. The default branch recovers an
  unknown state toward IDLE with enable low; startup still requires reset.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 08. Address alignment and two-cycle holds
- Required input sampling windows:

   Edge   b_wr_addr_i     a_wr_addr_i     y0_i / y1_i
   T      B for bundle j not sampled    both results for bundle j
   T+1    not sampled    A for bundle j not sampled

- B only needs to be stable around T and may change after its sampling edge.
  A only needs to be stable around T+1 and may change after that sampling edge.
- Holding B for two clocks and presenting A the next clock is sufficient when
  those windows cover T and T+1. Neither address requires an extra hold through
  the later memory-write edge: the writer has already registered it.
- B does not have to remain valid until A arrives. A does not have to be valid
  as early as T. These are separate address inputs with separate sampling times.
- Hold duration alone does not align transactions. The upstream address pipeline
  must align B to the accepted result strobe and A to the following clock.
  This contract applies after all read, multiplier, adder and connection delays.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 09. Command presentation versus memory consumption
- For one bundle and no reset or immediately following bundle:

   Edge   Inputs used         Command after edge    Memory consumes at edge
   T      valid, B, Y0, Y1    B / Y1, enable=1       preceding command, if any
   T+1    A                   A / saved Y0, en=1    B / Y1
   T+2    none                enable=0              A / saved Y0

- Two consecutive cycles with wr_en_o high represent two memory writes; memory
  must use the enable level each clock rather than detect only its rising edge.
- Idle output addresses/data hold the last command. Their values alone are not
  additional writes. There is no writer done_o or memory-commit acknowledgment.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 10. Why the Y0 holding register is required
// Source: ../../processing_elements/fft_adder_core/fft_adder_core.sv
"
                case(second_result_selector)
                    2'd0: y0r_o <= second_sum;
                    2'd1: y1r_o <= second_sum;
                    2'd2: y0i_o <= second_sum;
                    2'd3: begin
                        y1i_o <= second_sum;
                        valid_o <= 1;
                    end
                endcase
"
- Let V be the edge after which the adder's complete valid_o asserts. The current
  bundle's Y0r/Y1r/Y0i/Y1i registers update at V-3, V-2, V-1 and V, respectively.
- At the minimum input interval of four clocks, the next bundle's Y0r updates
  after V+1. With direct clocked connectivity, the writer accepts the adder's
  strobe at T=V+1 and sees the old coherent Y0 before that simultaneous update.
- The writer's second command is issued at T+1=V+2. Reading live y0_i then could
  combine the next bundle's real field with the prior bundle's imaginary field.
- Saving the whole Y0 word at T prevents this corruption. Y1 is captured into
  wr_data at T and therefore needs no separate holding register for this schedule.
- If the connection adds stages, align valid, result words and addresses through
  those stages together. The V-to-T relationship above assumes direct connection.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 11. Throughput, gaps and held valid
- A new bundle can be accepted at T+2 if valid_i was sampled low at T+1 and is
  high again at T+2. Its B command can follow the prior A command without a gap.
- This gives a writer minimum acceptance interval of two clocks. The existing
  FFT cores' minimum interval of four clocks leaves adequate write-port capacity.
- With longer or irregular result gaps, IDLE waits, enable stays low and stored
  data/address values hold. There is no free-running transaction assumption.
- valid_previous tracks valid_i each non-reset clock, including WRITE_A. Holding
  valid_i high through the return to IDLE does not launch another copy.
- No acceptance occurs in WRITE_A and no busy-time request is queued. A new
  transaction needs its own sampled low-to-high strobe while IDLE.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// 12. Reset, cancellation and limits of write atomicity
- rst_i must span a rising edge to clear state, valid history and registered
  enable. Its direct mask also suppresses wr_en_o while reset is asserted.
- Reset between T and T+1 can suppress the uncommitted B command and prevent
  issuance of the A command. Reset after A is issued can suppress its commitment
  if asserted before the memory's consuming edge.
- A write already consumed by memory cannot be undone. Reset after B commits
  but before A commits can leave only the B location updated. The two writes
  are not an atomic transaction; reset/restart handling belongs to the caller.
- Resetting this writer does not flush the arithmetic pipeline. Reset or otherwise
  invalidate upstream in-flight results consistently to prevent stale relaunches.
- No data or address clearing is required for correctness when enable is obeyed.
[Marc103 - 11/13/26]

