// 13. Simulation evidence - 11/13/26
- The recorded source hash matches the writer revision reviewed in this file.
- WSL Verilator passed four direct protocol configurations on the user's machine:

   DATA_WIDTH  ADDR_WIDTH  Checked clocks  Presented write commands
            1           1            1959                       624
           16           4            1959                       624
           37           7            1959                       624
           64          30            1959                       624

- Directed checks cover repeated bundles, minimum two-clock writer spacing,
  longer gaps, held-high valid, one-clock-later A availability, changing live data
  after acceptance, reset aborts and data/address holding during idle/reset.
- Questa passed the same direct cases and two integrations with the actual FFT
  adder, with all its optional arithmetic registers disabled/enabled. Public
  adder latencies were 10 and 30 clocks. Each integration checked 49 bundles,
  98 presented/consumed writes, continuous II=4 traffic and longer input gaps.
- Each actual-adder integration observed 39 occasions when live Y0 changed before
  the second writer command. The saved Y0 still supplied the expected data.
- The combined Verilator adder-integration elaboration stalled and was stopped;
  it is not a passing integration result. Actual-adder integration evidence is
  from Questa; WSL Verilator evidence covers the four direct writer cases.
- Evidence: simulate/fsm_fft_writer_checks/summary.json, contract/result.log,
  and questa/result.log. Temporary checker sources were removed after testing.
- This verifies serializer behavior and adder output-window compatibility.
  It does not verify a complete FFT memory/address-pipeline interconnection,
  numerical FFT results, read-after-write safety or downstream completion.
- Completed templated testbench evidence (same RTL revision, 11/13/26):
  The class-based reference schedules B/Y1 and A/saved-Y0 command deadlines,
  and separately predicts what memory consumes before each rising-edge update.
  The monitor samples that pre-edge write port independently of post-edge outputs.
  Both channels are compared on every clock, including idle/reset and across
